// `Iterator` -- a sequence produced one element at a time.
//
// **`hasNext` and `next`, not `next() -> Option<Item>`.** The conventional shape
// costs a payload-enum value per element, and Prismio's payload enums are not
// tagged unions yet -- so the conventional spelling would put an allocation in
// the innermost part of every loop. This pair answers the same two questions
// with no per-element value at all.
//
// `next` takes `inout self` because advancing is a mutation, which means an
// iterator must be a `mut` binding. That is the contract, and T18 makes it one:
// an implementation may not quietly take the receiver by borrow instead.
//
// `for x in it` desugars to exactly this protocol, so a type implementing
// `Iterator` is usable with the loop syntax and needs nothing else.

trait Iterator {
    type Item
    fn hasNext(self) -> Bool
    fn next(inout self) -> Self.Item
}
